WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:05.829
Five decades ago, Barry Rosenberg fell in love with the sights of Colorado.

2
00:00:07.559 --> 00:00:10.560
He moved here with the background, working and civil rights

3
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:11.569
for

4
00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:11.939
people

5
00:00:12.239 --> 00:00:16.260
and ready to change the city. Some people had never been to a movie theater before.

6
00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:20.479
He had his own van where he picked up people with disabilities to go to the theater,

7
00:00:20.489 --> 00:00:22.020
restaurants and rafting

8
00:00:22.149 --> 00:00:24.639
because they had no other way of getting around

9
00:00:24.649 --> 00:00:28.219
including public transportation in his world of advocacy.

10
00:00:28.229 --> 00:00:30.329
He knew that had to change.

11
00:00:30.729 --> 00:00:34.139
He worked alongside well known activist, Reverend Wade Blank,

12
00:00:34.895 --> 00:00:37.405
belief that the pen is mightier than the sword.

13
00:00:37.416 --> 00:00:40.846
And that's when folks started showing up at RTD meetings.

14
00:00:40.855 --> 00:00:44.085
This is 1974 and people with disabilities.

15
00:00:44.096 --> 00:00:48.805
You rarely see people in wheelchairs going anywhere and here's 50 people,

16
00:00:48.816 --> 00:00:51.326
but they only got a few specialized buses that

17
00:00:51.335 --> 00:00:54.245
ran very limited routes and it wasn't enough.

18
00:00:54.416 --> 00:00:55.046
They

19
00:00:55.335 --> 00:00:56.846
were not sympathetic,

20
00:00:57.145 --> 00:01:01.405
they kept reaching out, they kept writing but change kept stalling.

21
00:01:01.692 --> 00:01:05.921
Four years later, July 5th, 1978 no one knew

22
00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:06.202
what

23
00:01:06.372 --> 00:01:06.882
was going to

24
00:01:07.472 --> 00:01:08.921
transpire. Protesters shut down

25
00:01:09.071 --> 00:01:10.592
Colfax and Broadway first,

26
00:01:10.601 --> 00:01:14.232
there was a group of nine people in wheelchairs that blocked the front of the bus.

27
00:01:14.402 --> 00:01:18.211
Then another 11 protesters circled around a second bus.

28
00:01:18.232 --> 00:01:19.141
At that point,

29
00:01:19.152 --> 00:01:24.912
people had no choice but to pay attention to a group now known as the gang of 19.

30
00:01:24.921 --> 00:01:28.512
We tried the pen, we tried working together expressing our needs

31
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.559
and instead of the sword, this was a cannon shot.

32
00:01:32.889 --> 00:01:36.930
The protest lasted into the night as demonstrators faced off with police,

33
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:40.440
it stretched into the next day and RTD started listening,

34
00:01:40.449 --> 00:01:41.819
everything you did was worth it.

35
00:01:41.949 --> 00:01:44.599
You paved the way for accessibility. You changed the world.

36
00:01:44.629 --> 00:01:47.099
It was a pinnacle moment that both Rosenberg

37
00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.610
and Gabe Christie with RTD say helped pass the

38
00:01:50.139 --> 00:01:53.120
with Disabilities Act and changed RTD.

39
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.239
They made their fleet more accessible and to this day

40
00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.760
have changed the way they listen to the community.

41
00:01:58.769 --> 00:02:01.599
The gang of 19 protests are a result of RT not listening

42
00:02:01.889 --> 00:02:03.900
to our constituents, not listening to the people in our community.

43
00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.029
Now we have two advisory committees for persons with disabilities.

44
00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:14.160
46 years later, Rosenberg only knows of two original protesters still living

45
00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:14.880
50 years

46
00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:15.029
is

47
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:15.899
long time.

48
00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:16.210
But

49
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:16.610
feel

50
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:17.000
great

51
00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:17.320
about

52
00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.039
Bob and Renata Conrad who now live in

53
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:23.009
Nebraska and they know they changed the country.

54
00:02:23.029 --> 00:02:26.759
I was on the bus one day and this young kid came on the

55
00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:30.770
bus in a wheelchair and he just had this big grin on his face

56
00:02:31.139 --> 00:02:33.729
and I asked him what's going on? He said

57
00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:36.089
this is the first time in my life

58
00:02:36.259 --> 00:02:41.970
I've ever got to go anywhere without my mother. I had this whole sense of freedom.

59
00:02:41.979 --> 00:02:46.229
They've also seen access to restaurants change and representation on TV.

60
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.529
As well as a plaque in downtown Denver

61
00:02:48.539 --> 00:02:50.970
marking the spot where they changed the country.

62
00:02:50.979 --> 00:02:53.649
Someone said we were troublemakers and we said,

63
00:02:53.869 --> 00:02:54.690
good trouble.